 ## Overview
-Regularization losses are auxiliary objective terms added to the training of differentiable volume rendering models to constrain the solution space, prevent overfitting, and enforce physical or structural priors on the scene representation. While photometric losses (like MSE or L1) measure fidelity to input images, regularization losses operate on the latent parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions) or the rendered output's statistical properties to suppress artifacts such as "floaters," noise, and geometric inconsistencies [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. They set themselves apart from sibling methods like [Optimization Loops and Training Procedures](5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md) by defining *what* to penalize, whereas loops define *how* to update parameters.
+Regularization losses are auxiliary objective terms added to the training of differentiable volume rendering models to enforce physical plausibility, suppress artifacts (such as "floaters" or noise), and prevent overfitting to input data [S1, Siblings, unofficial]. They operate on scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions) or output metrics rather than just pixel-wise photometric error [S1, Siblings, unofficial]. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [Color Volume Optimization and Density Initialization](842-color-volume-optimization-and-density-initialization.md) (which focuses on starting conditions) and [Optimization Loops and Training Procedures](5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md) (which focuses on the solver schedule) by providing the specific mathematical penalties that guide the gradient descent [S1, Siblings, unofficial].
 
