 ## Background
-Regularization in volume rendering emerged alongside the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and differentiable rendering to address the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering problems. Early works like NeRF introduced noise injection and background modeling to mitigate artifacts [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments, such as Mip-NeRF, introduced proposal supervision and distortion losses to improve anti-aliasing and depth consistency [S3]. The field has since expanded to include geometric priors (Eikonal, Chamfer distance), uncertainty modeling, and specialized losses for Gaussian Splatting and tensorial representations like TensoRF [S6, unofficial]. These methods are now standard in modern radiance field optimization pipelines.
+Regularization in volume rendering emerged alongside the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and differentiable rendering to address the ill-posed nature of inverse problems, where multiple density distributions can produce identical color outputs [S1, unofficial]. Early works like Neural Volumes introduced Total Variation (TV) priors on voxel grids to suppress noise [S1, unofficial]. The Distortion Loss was later introduced to specifically target "floaters" and depth ambiguity in continuous radiance fields [S1, unofficial]. More recent approaches incorporate perceptual losses [S1, unofficial], uncertainty modeling [S1, unofficial][S2], and geometric constraints like Chamfer Distance [S1, unofficial] to improve both visual fidelity and geometric accuracy. The field has also seen the development of specialized losses for specific representations, such as Gaussian Splatting [S4, unofficial] and Tensorial Radiance Fields (TensoRF) [S5, unofficial].
 
